# rp2350_geek

A Waveshare RP2350-GEEK: an RP2350A in the RP2040-GEEK's USB-stick case, with
16MB of flash by Waveshare's description and the SDK's header, carrying the
same 1.14 inch 240 by 135 IPS panel on an ST7789 and TF card socket, with its
native USB port on a USB-A plug.

It derives from pico2-arm because that is what it is: the same RP2350A the
Pico SDK configures as pico2. As with rp2040_geek, the SDK's
waveshare_rp2350_geek header is outside the bridge's vendor vocabulary, so the
board derives and the board table corrects. The chip, the SDK, and the ABI
come from pico2-arm; the flash beyond pico2's 4MB goes unused.

Its wiring is the RP2040-GEEK's, pin for pin: the panel on SPI1 with DC GP8,
CS GP9, clock GP10, data GP11, reset GP12, and the backlight on GP25 through
an NPN switch; the TF socket on GP18 to GP23, CS GP23 in SPI mode; the I2C
header on GP28 and GP29; the UART header on GP4 and GP5; and the header
labelled SWD on GP2 and GP3.

Its three-pin headers follow the Raspberry Pi Debug Probe's ports as the
RP2040-GEEK's do: the same JST-SH connector and order, TX, GND, RX and
SWCLK, GND, SWDIO, so a Debug Probe's UART port on the UART header is the
console. The SWD header is GP2 and GP3 here too, not the RP2350's SWCLK and
SWDIO, which reach no header or test point on the schematic; it drives SWD
when the board runs debug-probe firmware, and a probe plugged into it does
not reach this chip. The openocd runner and debugger accept the board, since
it inherits pico2-arm's rp2350 machine, but need the chip's own debug port.

The board table replaces the same two pico2 defaults it replaces for
rp2040_geek: no LED, GP25 being the backlight, and the default UART as UART1
on GP4 and GP5. ADC_AVDD is the 3V3 rail, so the ADC reference is 3300 mV.

Every pin above was read from Waveshare's schematic, RP2350-GEEK.pdf. Its
flash symbol is labelled W25Q32, a 4MB part, against the 16MB the product page
and the SDK state; deriving from pico2-arm uses 4MB and is right either way.
Waveshare's own LCD demo for this board names GP13 as the backlight, carried
over from the Pico-LCD-1.14 it was written for; the schematic's GP25 is what
is used here. None of it is qualified on hardware.

https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/RP2350-GEEK
